# Workspace Governance

*verified-against: 3.0 (2026-07-23)*

This document is the plain-English map of how the machine's workspace — the folder that holds
every repo, clone, and scratch environment a session touches — is organized, so that work never
has to guess where a new folder belongs. It exists because the alternative is what actually
happened before it: dozens of loose folders at the workspace root (stale worktrees, QA clones,
one-off probes, loose extraction screenshots), because nothing had ever said otherwise and every
session filled the gap its own way.

You never need to read code to use this document. If a term below is unfamiliar, its
explanation is right where it's used.

---

## The four zones

Everything at the workspace root belongs to exactly one of four folders. Nothing else lives at
the root — not a stray clone, not a one-off test folder, not a leftover from an old session.

- **`repos/`** — permanent clones. One folder per real repository, the ones you'd recognize by
  name and expect to still be there next year.
- **`worktrees/`** — managed **git worktrees** (a git worktree is a second, linked working
  copy of a repo, checked out to a different branch, that shares the same underlying history —
  used so a session can work on one branch without disturbing another). These are expected to
  come and go.
- **`scratch/`** — everything disposable: probes, throwaway QA environments, one-off ports of a
  repo for a single experiment. Scratch folders are NEVER created inside a repo — always here,
  at the workspace root, where they're visible and where the housekeeping described below can
  find them.
- **`archive/`** — cold storage: things worth keeping but no longer active. See "Archive and
  retention" below.

If a session is about to create a folder at the workspace root and it doesn't fit one of these
four, that's the signal something is wrong with the plan, not a reason to invent a fifth zone.

## Naming conventions

Names carry the information a human needs at a glance, without opening the folder:

- **Worktrees:** `<repo>--<purpose>--<YYYYMMDD>` — e.g. `myproject-fork--session-c-build--20260723`.
- **Scratch folders:** `<purpose>--<YYYYMMDD>` — e.g. `port-verify-probe--20260723`.

The date is the creation date, not a deadline — the deadline lives in the birth certificate
(next section).

## Birth certificates: WHY.md

Every worktree or scratch folder is created **together with** a one-line `WHY.md` file at its
root, answering four questions:

- **Purpose** — why this folder exists, in one sentence.
- **Created** — the date it was made.
- **Expected lifetime** — how long it's expected to be needed (a duration or an end date).
- **Creating session/plan** — what session, plan, or task made it, so a later reader can trace
  back to the decision that started it.

**Creating a worktree or scratch folder without a `WHY.md` is itself the defect** — not a
missing nice-to-have. A folder with no birth certificate is exactly the kind of thing that
becomes unrecognizable clutter six months later, because nothing recorded why it was ever
there.

## Death dates and the reaper (report-only, never automatic deletion)

The housekeeping routine (`/sweep`) gains a workspace-hygiene check that looks for:

- worktrees whose branch has been merged or deleted,
- scratch folders past the lifetime stated in their own `WHY.md`,
- root folders that sit in none of the four zones and aren't listed in the registry
  (`projects.yaml`, below).

It reports these in one plain-English list, with sizes, so a human can see at a glance what's
grown stale.

**DELETION IS ALWAYS A HUMAN WORD.** The reaper's whole job stops at noticing — it never
deletes, moves, or archives anything on its own. Noticing is automatic; removing is not. This
matches the same append-only, never-delete-without-a-human principle that governs everything
else this harness writes.

## Evidence placement

Artifacts captured while extracting or probing a live surface — screenshots, accessibility
dumps, probe logs — live at:

```
manifests/<surface>/evidence/<run-date>/
```

right beside the manifest rows they're evidence for, instead of scattered loose at a folder
root (the "55 loose `state-*.png` screenshots" problem this convention exists to prevent).

This is specifically for **extraction evidence** (proving a behavioral-manifest row). It does
not move or rename **engine evidence** — the execution engine's own evidence keeps its existing
home at `deploy/evidence/`. Two different kinds of evidence, two different homes; neither
displaces the other.

## The registry: projects.yaml

A single file, `projects.yaml`, at the workspace root (one level above `repos/`), lists every
project the workspace holds:

```yaml
projects:
  - name: <short project name>
    path: <path relative to the workspace root>
    role: control-plane | corpus | port | fork | dormant
    notes: <optional free text>
```

The four roles, in plain terms:

- **control-plane** — the project that runs the actual operation (issues decisions, owns the
  live system).
- **corpus** — a knowledge base or wiki project observed by a control-plane project, without
  running the operation itself.
- **port** — a working copy of another project's code, adapted or relocated for a specific
  purpose.
- **fork** — a working copy tracking an upstream template or source, meant to receive its
  updates over time.
- **dormant** — still present, not currently active; kept rather than deleted.

**One registry, two readers.** The reaper (above) treats anything at the workspace root that
isn't in a zone and isn't in this registry as unknown and flags it. Later, a cross-project
rollup desk (tracked separately, v3.0-40) reads the same file to know what projects exist across
the whole workspace. Both read the same file so the two never drift apart.

The template ships a commented example at `projects.yaml.example`, beside this document.

## Archive and retention

`archive/` holds anything worth keeping that is no longer touched — a completed worktree, a
retired scratch probe whose output still has audit value, an old corpus snapshot nobody works
in anymore but nobody wants gone either.

**What moves there:** anything a `WHY.md` lifetime has expired on, if the operator says keep it
rather than delete it — or anything an operator explicitly names for archiving at any other
time. Nothing moves to `archive/` on a schedule or by inference; an item gets there because a
human decided it was worth keeping.

**When:** either at the moment its `WHY.md` "expected lifetime" passes and the operator chooses
"keep" over "let it go," or at the operator's word, any time.

**The index:** every item moved to `archive/` gets one line in `archive/ARCHIVE-INDEX.md` —
what it is, where it came from, and when it was archived. One line per item is enough; the
point is that nothing sits in `archive/` unexplained.

## Adopting this on a workspace that already has clutter

Turning this on for a workspace that already has folders scattered at its root does **not**
start with a reorganization. It starts with a report: the reaper runs first in report-only mode
over everything that exists today, classifying each folder by best guess (which zone it looks
like it belongs to, or "unrecognized"). A human reads that report and decides, folder by
folder, what happens next — keep in place, register, move to a zone, or archive. Only after
that operator-worded pass does anything actually move.

**Never a big-bang, machine-run reorganization.** The report comes first, every time — adopting
this convention on a live, messy workspace is itself a report-first migration, not an exception
to one.
